@@ -0,0 +1,52 @@
+VERDICT: Treat the reported worker “sleep” as observable progress loss: two synchronized five-worker freezes, retrying workers with live child jobs, and wasteful Docker polling need different fixes.
+
+Paths below are relative to `/Users/mac/Desktop/Terminal Bench/` unless prefixed `OUT/`. This is a read-only study. `OUT/scripts/audit_sessions.py` and `OUT/scripts/build_cases.py` reproduce the CSVs from the OpenCode DB opened with `mode=ro`; no worker, Docker job, task, or control file was changed. `S6_auto_speed/INCIDENTS.csv` already covers 20 run-level incidents; the 20 rows in `OUT/SLEEP_CASES.csv` are **session-level episodes**, not 20 new run incidents.
+
+## 1. Sources and coverage
+
+**Finding.** There are nine `auto/runs/*/events.jsonl` files with 308 events and nine corresponding reports; the archived `20261025_0048_night` has `state.json` but no event/report pair (command: `glob('auto/runs/*/events.jsonl')`, count 9; `sum(lines)`, 308; `wc -l auto/runs/*/{REPORT.md,events.jsonl,state.json}`). The DB query in `OUT/scripts/audit_sessions.py:20-27` found 175 seven-character SHA sessions in its time range, of which 93 appear in saved auto run `state.json#/workers`. `OUT/POLL_STATS.csv:1` describes the full session lifetime, including time before adoption or after an auto stop; filter nonempty `auto_runs` to obtain the 93. Board results and statuses are checked by `OUT/scripts/audit_sessions.py:18-19`; `decisions.jsonl:443-450,504-505` records the relevant run overrides and stop decisions. All nine reports and event files were searched for retry, stall, doom, stop, and Docker terms; the prior S6 inventory was used as the baseline (`master_chat_work/20261030_upgrade2/S6_auto_speed/FINDINGS.md:22-35`).
+
+**What to change / expected gain / risk / check.** Keep an immutable per-run worker index (`sha`, session ID, prompt, expected deliverable, OUT, child-job manifest) in `auto/runs/<run>/`; the current `state.json#/workers` lacks child identity and historical transition timestamps. This makes later sleep attribution exact and avoids guesswork about children. Risk: stale PID reuse; store process start time and job path, and never act on a PID alone. Check by replaying the nine saved states into a read-only audit and reconciling every `spawn`/`adopt` event to one worker row. This is a proposed morning change, not a change made here.
+
+## 2. “Went to sleep”: every identified auto episode
+
+**Finding.** `OUT/SLEEP_CASES.csv:2-11` records ten workers in two synchronized freezes. Five sessions stopped issuing tools at 23:19-23:22 and resumed at 02:50 (207-212 minutes each); another five stopped at 04:59 and resumed at 07:50 (170-172 minutes each). The first freeze is absent from the run report's stall discussion, which only names 05:00-07:50 (`auto/runs/20261027T2239_overnight/REPORT.md:5,30,80`). The event stream also has 23:14→02:53 and 04:58→07:52 delivery gaps (`auto/runs/20261027T2239_overnight/events.jsonl:15-18,46-49`). Calendar loss is about **6 h 22 m** across the two windows; summed worker idle is about **31.8 worker-hours**, and those are overlapping measures. The exact DB evidence for each worker is `SLEEP_CASES.csv#/evidence`: an assistant message `time_created` followed by a tool `state.time.start` more than 170 minutes later. The transcripts show work was incomplete: 4e298da was still writing Gate 03 of QC, 9a85fdc had a failed write then continued its fix, f0331ad was editing, and 7d4de68 was still at QC setup (`python3 OUT/scripts/timeline.py <sha> <start> <end>`; `auto/runs/20261027T2239_overnight/REPORT.md:13-14,26-30`). 361674d explicitly said, “Waiting for nop, stub, forgery” before the second gap; its Step 10 job continued (`opencode.db session.title=361674d, message.time_created=2026-10-28 04:59:10 IST, next part.state.time.start=07:50:08`).
+
+**Cause.** **UNVERIFIED.** These were not ten independent worker decisions to finish a turn: their assistant messages had `finish=tool-calls`, and the watcher delivered no events in the same windows. The second run report says “cause unknown (provider or host)” and “no watcher PAUSED/NETWORK event, no Docker containers” (`REPORT.md:30`). `pmset -g log` showed a live `caffeinate` `PreventSystemSleep` assertion at 02:42 on 28 Oct, so a normal idle system sleep is not established by these records (command: `pmset -g log | rg '^2026-10-28 02:42' | rg 'caffeinate.*PreventSystemSleep'`). **INFERRED:** a shared provider/runtime/host scheduling pause is more plausible than ten identical worker-level mistakes; exact layer remains unknown.
+
+**Other stops.** `SLEEP_CASES.csv:12-21` adds: two smoke workers in invisible rate-limit retry until Ayush aborted them (5.4 min each, `auto/runs/20261027T0107_smoketest/REPORT.md:4,14`); two production workers in provider retry from ~17:44 to Ayush's 18:55 stop while a Step 10 chain finished and cost containers kept running (about 71 min each, `auto/runs/20261028T1553_evening/REPORT.md:5,49`); ed621c7's network retry (3.3 min, `auto/runs/20261029T0355_night/events.jsonl:18-19`); two doom-loop guard stops (2.3 and 1.2 min, `auto/runs/20261028T1553_evening/events.jsonl:9-10`, `auto/runs/20261029T0355_night/events.jsonl:31-32`); 6740572's `ContextOverflowError` after compression #34 (1.4 min, DB `message.error.name` and next user message at 03:36:44); 4989b35's deliberate 10:23 abort while s3/s5/gate3 children continued to 10:27 and a 67.1 min gap before the master's continuation (`auto/runs/20261030T0100_night/REPORT.md:35-38`); and 361674d's content-filter stop, resumed by a master send (`auto/runs/20261027T2239_overnight/events.jsonl:17-18`). Two `finish=length` records exist in SHA sessions before the auto runs (98c5c50, 60e9db0), but **no `finish=length` stop was found inside these nine auto runs** (DB query: `session.title`, `message.data.finish='length'`, 26-30 Oct; output in audit notes). The 6740572 context error is the verified in-run context-cap case. Compaction was the next attempted tool for 217875e, dcd21a3, and 7d4de68 during the shared freezes, while other frozen workers had bash/edit/read next; **compaction as a root cause is UNVERIFIED** (`SLEEP_CASES.csv:2-11`, DB `part.data#type/tool`).
+
+**Clean-final test.** The 121 completed assistant final turns of auto-linked SHA sessions are listed in `OUT/FINAL_TURNS.csv` (command: `python3 OUT/scripts/audit_sessions.py`, then `wc -l OUT/FINAL_TURNS.csv` = 122 including header). I found **no verified case** where a worker intentionally ended a clean final with its own background job still running. The confirmed outliving children followed a manual abort, and the retry cases did not complete an assistant final. This distinction matters because “never end your turn while your own job runs” already appears in `_COMMON_RULES_v5.md:35`.
+
+**What to change / expected gain / risk / check.** Add a per-worker `pending_job` and `expected_deliverable` record before every `nohup` launch, with an explicit completion sentinel and heartbeat; record a final turn as complete only after that sentinel and the result file exist. It would expose retry/abort orphaned work and prevent duplicate gate launches; no gain can be assigned to the two shared freezes without establishing their cause. Risk: stale or spoofed sentinels; bind to exact command, process start, input tree hash, and output path. Check with a fake detached job that completes after its worker is interrupted; the watcher should report `JOB_RUNNING` then `JOB_DONE` once, without rerunning or stopping it.
+
+## 3. Polling and Docker waits
+
+**Finding.** Across the 93 auto-linked SHA sessions, `OUT/POLL_STATS.csv` has **987 sleep-bearing tool calls**, **2,183.7 summed tool minutes (36.4 h)**, **240 sleep calls containing `docker ps`**, and **40 exact consecutive repeat sleep calls** (command: `python3 OUT/scripts/audit_sessions.py`, filter `auto_runs != ''`, sum columns). These are overlapping session/tool times, not 36.4 hours recoverable from one calendar night. The prior, broader 164-session study used a narrower poll definition and measured 55.5 h / 23% of aggregate session time, with 382 `sleep ...; docker ps` calls on 26-30 Oct (`master_chat_work/20261030_speed_measure/MEASURE.md:79-108`). High examples are 4989b35: 66 sleeps/164.8 min (`POLL_STATS.csv:166`), 6740572: 114/157.4 (`:150`), and 361674d: 46/104.4 (`:79`). The CSV's `explicit_slot_wait_calls` is a conservative text-in-same-message signal, **not a complete slot-wait count**; 733 calls had own-job context and 236 were unclear by that heuristic (script `audit_sessions.py:43-60`).
+
+**Slot versus own job.** ed621c7 explicitly waited for *another session's* container before launching its first own one: 09:19 to 11:01, at least 102 minutes, with 29 Docker samples and only 1-2 foreign containers (prior primary DB review in `MEASURE.md:123-132`; `POLL_STATS.csv:127` has 48 sleeps, 108.0 min, 37 containing `docker ps`). Cold-chain had a separately logged 5 m 47 s wait for a nop slot (`MEASURE.md:132-134`). By contrast ac12c0b, cc541be, 361674d, and 4989b35 repeatedly tailed **their own** Step 10/scoring logs while detached chains ran (`opencode.db` commands reproduced by `OUT/scripts/timeline.py`; `POLL_STATS.csv:79,108,131,166`; `auto/runs/20261028T1553_evening/REPORT.md:42,49`). The identical `sleep 235; date; tail...` call was denied three times in ac12c0b's transcript at 16:53, and the same pattern recurred for cc541be at 06:43; the guard stopped the assistant but not the chain (`events.jsonl` citations above).
+
+**What a blocking wait would save.** A single event/log-completion wait **per background phase** could replace repeated assistant-issued `sleep; tail` calls and eliminate the exact-call guard exposure. It would not shorten the underlying Harbor/verifier runtime, so **wall-time saving is UNVERIFIED**; the observed 36.4 h is only an upper bound on tool occupation across concurrent sessions. A `docker events` wait for a *foreign* slot is insufficient by itself: ed621c7 was obeying a social serial rule even with 1-2 containers up; it needs shared capacity admission/lease rather than another poll mechanism (`MEASURE.md:123-134`, `S6_auto_speed/FINDINGS.md:31-35`). Check with a simulated 20-minute log writer: compare worker tool calls, end-to-end completion, correct exit/status capture, and a stale/missed event fallback. A too-long blocking call can hit tool timeout or hide a dead process; use a bounded wait plus heartbeat/fallback.
+
+## 4. Watcher coverage and gaps
+
+**Finding.** Current `auto.py` polls at 60 s, calls `poll_workers`, `poll_harness`, `check_limit`, then delivers events (`auto.py:68-79,1894-1945`). `poll_workers` treats server `retry` separately, waits two idle polls before `handle_result`, and reads passive adopted sessions through DB (`auto.py:1401-1455`). `check_limit` recognizes worker/MQ usage limits, holds new work and the queue, notifies, and clears after two clean polls (`auto.py:1515-1589`). `handle_doom` sends one corrective continue, then blocks a second guard stop (`auto.py:1376-1398`). `note_master_hold` alerts only when queued watcher events meet a master in provider retry for two polls; it deliberately ignores a merely busy master (`auto.py:1767-1792`). These cover the later doom episode and status-visible limits, but the 27 Oct smoke run shows the older watcher missed an internally retrying, apparently BUSY session (`REPORT.md:14-17`); **production efficacy of the current retry status path remains UNVERIFIED**.
+
+`check_stall` emits only when **every** active owned worker's newest activity is older than 20 minutes; its activity clock includes last send, last assistant message, and latest OUT mtime (`auto.py:1480-1512`). A single idle worker beside active peers is invisible. `check_gap` detects watcher silence over 10 minutes only when the watcher gets another poll (`auto.py:1712-1718`); it could have reported both shared freezes after 02:50 and 07:50, but could not wake the watcher while it was silent. The old run produced neither stall nor gap event during its 05:00-07:50 freeze (`REPORT.md:30,80,88`). The current `poll_workers` also reports an idle assistant as `done` after two polls without consulting a detached-job manifest (`auto.py:1431-1445`), and `handle_result` marks a nonerror response done without verifying the expected deliverable (`auto.py:1346-1358`). `ContextOverflowError` falls into the generic nonretry error path (`auto.py:553-565,1359-1367`); 6740572 needed an external continuation.
+
+**Exact catch rules.** (a) If an owned worker has no new assistant tool start/completion **and** no OUT or job heartbeat for 20 minutes while its expected deliverable is missing, emit `WORKER_STALL <sha>` even when other workers progress; do not flag a known live long tool or a job whose heartbeat advances. (b) If an idle/errored worker has a verified child manifest entry still alive, emit `JOB_RUNNING_WITH_IDLE_WORKER`, preserve the job, and defer `done` until the completion sentinel/result is read. (c) If a worker is `busy` with an empty assistant for two polls and the worker-server retry state or local provider log identifies a limit, apply `LIMIT`; otherwise emit `UNKNOWN_RETRY` without holding the queue. (d) On `ContextOverflowError`, emit `CONTEXT_STOP` and allow one checkpoint-based continuation after re-reading TODO/LOG; a second error stops. (e) On `check_gap` over 10 minutes, record the exact elapsed gap and all workers with missing deliverables before any wind-down decision; an **independent** watchdog is needed if detection must occur while `auto.py` itself is silent. Each rule is narrower than “busy for a long time” and maps respectively to the individual stall blind spot, orphan jobs, smoke retry, 6740572, and the two shared freezes.
+
+**What to change / expected gain / risk / check.** Implement the rules near `auto.py:1401-1512,1712-1718,1894-1945`. Expected gain: minutes to hours of earlier detection when the watcher is alive, and accurate post-gap accounting; no guaranteed rescue while the whole runtime is paused. Risks: false alerts on legitimate long tools, stale file mtimes, and duplicate continuations. Replay the saved DB/event timelines with a fake clock: one live worker plus one 21-minute idle worker must alert; a heartbeat job must not; the 23:19 and 04:59 gaps must each yield one GAP on return; the 6740572 error must continue once only; retry clean-up must leave the queue held until two clean polls. External watchdog installation is a separate operational decision.
+
+## 5. Ranked changes for morning implementation
+
+| Rank | Exact change and location | Expected gain | What it could break | Proof before enablement |
+|---|---|---|---|---|
+| 1 | Replace `_COMMON_RULES_v5.md:35,50` text with: “For each detached job, write a manifest with input tree, log, PID/start time, expected result and completion sentinel. Wait for that sentinel with a bounded blocking tool call; after a timeout, check the process and log once. Do not start the same job twice. Report the final result only after the sentinel and result exist.” Put the same rule into the P11/P12 worker templates that launch Step 10 (`prompts/templates/P11_full_qc_v3.md:76-105`, P12 issued/template equivalent). | Removes most repeated own-job polls and guard stops; measured exposure 987 sleeps/36.4 aggregate tool-hours, actual wall gain **UNVERIFIED**. | Tool timeout, false sentinel, missed job failure. | Fake chain: success, nonzero exit, hung child, lost sentinel; count tool calls and verify exact tree/result. |
+| 2 | In `auto.py:1401-1512` add per-worker progress and detached-job state, the `WORKER_STALL` and `JOB_RUNNING_WITH_IDLE_WORKER` rules in section 4; keep `handle_result` from marking `done` before expected result exists (`:1346-1358`). | Catches a single sleeping worker and orphaned Docker work even if peers remain active. | False positive during a legitimate long tool; premature duplicate work if result path is wrong. | Replay 20 rows in `SLEEP_CASES.csv`; live-heartbeat control produces no alert; same-tree result accepted exactly once. |
+| 3 | Use a shared Docker admission lease for foreign-slot waits, as proposed in `master_chat_work/20261030_upgrade2/S6_auto_speed/FINDINGS.md:31-35`; update `AUTO_POLICY.md:47` and worker templates so a free capacity lease starts a job without waiting for Docker to become empty. | Targets ed621c7's verified 102-minute slot wait; gain on other sessions **UNVERIFIED**. | Memory overcommit or paid-run starvation. | Same-tree one/three-lane benchmark plus mixed paid/worker dry run with 8 GiB/4 GiB resource claims and stale-lease recovery. |
+| 4 | Add independent watcher heartbeat/restart/alert around `auto.py:1712-1718,1894-1945`; `check_gap` must audit all pending workers on resume. | Would identify the missed 23:19-02:50 gap and logged 05:00-07:50 gap promptly **after** resumption; an independent watchdog may alert during a watcher-only stall. | Duplicate watchers or false restart during host suspension. | Freeze only watcher process in a test fixture, then simulated host-wide pause; assert one leader, one GAP per episode, no duplicate sends. |
+| 5 | Strengthen retry/context handling at `auto.py:553-565,1359-1367,1409-1413,1515-1589`: empty-assistant limit fallback, `CONTEXT_STOP` once, preserve child manifests. Keep the existing one-shot doom continuation (`:1376-1398`). | Prevents smoke-style invisible retry and short context/guard stops; may reduce a 71-minute missed retry window. | Misclassifying a quiet tool or network error as quota; repeat after lost checkpoint. | Reproduce t1/t2 BUSY-empty, ac12/a4f retry, cc541be doom, and 6740572 overflow with fake status/logs. |
+| 6 | Add stop/resume ownership report to `auto.py:1113-1139` and the worker template: list each verified child job, its tree, and resume command; never infer ownership from a task prefix. | Avoids 4989b35's lost rescoring after manual abort; recorded restart added ~15-20 min (`auto/runs/20261030T0100_night/REPORT.md:37-38`). | PID reuse; accidental job termination. | Fake two same-task jobs, abort one worker, verify neither job is killed automatically and only its own manifest is shown. |
+
+No tasks or code were changed tonight. The scripts and CSVs are analysis artifacts in OUT only.
